// Builds "Non-JD Order Tool - User Guide.docx" for Ben, Shannon, Barry and Curt.
// Facts come from the app (nonjd-order-app 0.11.0: src/engine, src/pages, src/returns, src/oneTime, src/cleanup,
// README), ../REORDER-RULES.md, ../RETURNS-AND-SCRAP.md and ../CLEANUP-CHECKS.md. Section 9 (One-Time templates, 0.10.0)
// follows the app's Template card (src/pages/oneTime), Templates page and src/oneTime/templates.ts; its example template
// (Cotton pre-season) is made up, because Prod had no templates at release.
// Section 7 (Cleanup checks, 0.11.0) follows src/pages/cleanup (CleanupChecksPage, CheckResultsPage, CheckEditorPage,
// ConditionRowEditor, MinimumsPage, builtIns.ts, checkResults.ts) and src/cleanup (fields.ts, conditions.ts). Counts
// are Prod of 2026-11-02 (v5 publish, data as of 22:04 UTC): Minimums with no demand 3,592, Stock value with no
// quantity 415. The saved-check example (On hand > 0, Sales 24 months = 0, Cost > 100) is Brian's real test, deleted.
// Section 10 (Inventory reports, 0.12.0) follows nonjd-order-app 0.12.0 (commit 46812b4: src/pages/inventory/*,
// src/inventory/builtIns.ts, fields.ts, reportResults.ts) and ../INVENTORY-REPORTS.md. Counts are Prod of 2026-11-03
// (v6 publish, 250,867 JD parts at 27 branches, data as of 19:42 UTC): MLPF Gaps 10,090. The copy example (Corp
// demands ≥ 4) is Brian's real test "Test of MLPF Gaps", deleted.
// Section 11 (Border Stores and 2 & 4 Cotton, 0.13.0) follows nonjd-order-app 0.13.0 (commit 1319ca9:
// src/inventory/builtIns.ts, report.ts, fields.ts, src/pages/inventory/ReportEditorPage.tsx, src/pages/cleanup/
// GroupRowEditor.tsx). Counts are Prod of 2026-11-04 (v7 publish, 250,911 active and 643,034 idle JD part records,
// data as of 15:43 UTC): Border Stores 1,576, 2 & 4 Cotton 251, MLPF Gaps 10,135. The copy example (branch one of 7,
// 14, parts with no activity on, 1,027 parts) is Brian's real test "Test 0.13.0", deleted.
// Export (N) on Transfer orders (0.13.1) follows nonjd-order-app 0.13.1 (commit 0a0d6e6: src/transfers/bulkExport.ts,
// src/export/csv.ts transferOrdersCsv, src/pages/transfers/TransferOrdersList.tsx).
// Section 12 (Corporate Stocking, 0.14.0) follows nonjd-order-app 0.14.0 (commit 15ca00a: src/inventory/builtIns.ts,
// partReport.ts, partFields.ts, partResults.ts, src/pages/inventory/PartReportResultsPage.tsx, PartRecordsPanel.tsx,
// ReportEditorPage.tsx, InventoryReportsPage.tsx). Counts are Prod of 2026-11-05 (251,019 active and 643,030 idle JD
// part records, demand window to 06:42 Central): Corporate Stocking 1,815. The copy example (Branches with demand
// >= 4, 618 parts) is Brian's real test "TEST Corporate Stocking 4+", deleted.
// Section 13 (Stock on superseded parts, 0.15.0) follows nonjd-order-app 0.15.0 (commit 130b68e: src/inventory/builtIns.ts,
// fields.ts, src/cleanup/conditions.ts, fields.ts, src/pages/cleanup/builtIns.ts). Counts are Prod of 2026-11-05: JD 2,477
// records at 20 branches ($571,028.49), non-JD 698 at 19 branches ($497,322.78; 22 with a word Super To).
// Worked examples: Dev Gold Fact_NonJD_Reorder built 2026-10-26 (window Oct 2025 - Sep 2026); the transfer example
// is the app's mock data (round numbers), as in REORDER-RULES.md. The Returns & scrap example (section 8) is Brian's Prod
// test of 2026-11-02: rule "Scrap default" (All franchises, no sales 36 months, unit cost at least $0.10), 49 AM parts.
// Run: npm install, then node guide.js [output file]
const { build, CONTENT_W } = require('./lib');

const w = (...fr) => fr.map((f) => Math.round(CONTENT_W * f));

build(process.argv[2] || 'Non-JD Order Tool - User Guide.docx', {
  title: 'SPI Parts Hub: user guide',
  subtitle: 'Formerly the Non-JD Parts Order Tool · For Ben, Shannon, Barry and Curt · Version 0.15.0 · November 5, 2026 (test version) · https://10.110.100.13:8443',
  footer: 'SPI Parts Hub: user guide (version 0.15.0)',
  blocks: [
    { p: '**SPI Parts Hub** (until 0.8.0 the Non-JD Parts Order Tool) opens on a launch page where you pick what to work on: **Non-JD ordering**, **Cleanup**, **Returns & scrap**, **Part Lookup** or **Inventory management** today, with **JD ordering** to come. Most of this guide is about Non-JD ordering.' },
    { p: '**In one sentence:** Non-JD ordering shows each branch which non-John Deere parts to reorder and why, suggests stock other branches could send instead, lets you tune the rules behind those suggestions, and turns the parts you pick into an order file for each vendor.' },
    { p: 'This guide covers getting in, what each page does, and exactly how every number is worked out. Version 0.8.0 includes Ben’s round 1 answers, several branches and dealer groups at once, **phase-in**, **transfer suggestions** and **saving transfers** in the app, with transfer orders and transfer rules, and **transfers on One-Time Orders** (round 2, from Ben’s answers of October 27), the **launch page** in front of it all (0.8.0), **returns and scrap** (0.9.0, section 8), **One-Time templates** (0.10.0, section 9), **Cleanup checks** (0.11.0, section 7), **Inventory reports** (0.12.0, section 10), **Border Stores** and **2 & 4 Cotton** (0.13.0, section 11), exporting **several transfer orders at once** (0.13.1, under Transfers), and **Corporate Stocking**, the first report with one row per part (0.14.0, section 12). What changed in each version is in the **walkthrough**, Part 2.' },

    { h1: '1. Getting started' },
    { steps: [
      'Open **https://10.110.100.13:8443** in Edge or Chrome on your PC.',
      'The first time, the browser warns "Your connection isn’t private". This test server uses its own certificate. Click **Advanced**, then **Continue to 10.110.100.13**. You only need to do this once.',
      'Sign in with your normal Microsoft (work) account. Only Ben, Shannon, Barry, Curt and Brian have access during the test.',
    ] },
    { p: 'Sign-in is shared by all your tabs and normally lasts until you close the browser. The sign-out button is next to your name at the bottom left. The version number is under **App Settings → About**.' },
    { h3: 'How fresh is the data?' },
    { p: 'The tool works from a snapshot of Equip taken in the morning: stock, sales history, open purchase orders and transfers. The date at the top right of every page says when it was taken. It turns **amber** when the data is more than one business day old. During this test the snapshot may be refreshed less often than daily, so check the date.' },
    { p: 'Your **rule** and **part setting** changes are different: they apply in the tool immediately, for everyone. The morning snapshot picks them up the next day.' },
    { p: 'Branches **12, 5, 97, 4B and 93C** are left out of the tool, as Ben asked.' },

    { h1: '2. The pages' },
    { h2: 'The launch page and areas (0.8.0)' },
    { p: 'The app opens on the **launch page**: the SPI logo, a greeting, a **Branch** select (your App Settings default to start with) and the date of the morning data. Under it is a card for each area. The cards say what is waiting at the picked branch:' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Non-JD ordering:** reorder, One-Time Orders and their templates, transfers and rules. It shows the same figures as its Home page, for example “220 parts to order · $93,000” and, in purple, “67 could come by transfer”.',
      '**Cleanup:** checks that list parts to fix in Equip (0.11.0, section 7). The card counts only the minimums with no demand at the picked branch, for example “21 minimums with no demand at Branch 1” (the same count as **Minimums with no demand** filtered to that branch).',
      '**Returns & scrap** (0.9.0): vendor returns and scrap lists, for example “1 open return · 2 open scrap lists” (section 8).',
      '**Part Lookup:** any part’s stock, prices, history and settings.',
      '**Inventory management** (0.12.0): the Corp Parts reports over the JD parts, with **MLPF Gaps**, **Border Stores** and **2 & 4 Cotton** (sections 10 and 11). Its card shows no count.',
      '**JD ordering** is greyed out with **Coming later**: it shows where the tool is heading and can’t be opened yet.',
    ] },
    { p: 'A count is left out when it is 0, while the data loads, or when the data can’t be read; the cards still work. Under the cards, **Other parts tools** links open **Parts Availability** and the **Parts reports** app in a new tab, next to **App Settings**.' },
    { p: 'Inside an area, the **menu on the left** shows only that area’s pages. At the top are **‹ All areas** (back to the launch page) and the **Area** select, which opens another area. If leaving would lose ticked parts or unsaved transfers, the app asks first. The breadcrumb at the top starts with the area, for example “Non-JD ordering › Recommended Reorder”. App Settings stays at the bottom of the menu. The branch you’re looking at shows at the top right. Cleanup has one menu item, **Checks** (0.11.0); Inventory management has one, **Reports** (0.12.0).' },
    { p: 'Every address from before 0.8.0 still opens the same page, so bookmarks keep working. The only change: the bare address is now the launch page, and Non-JD ordering’s Home is at **/non-jd**.' },

    { h2: 'Home (Non-JD ordering)' },
    { p: 'The first page of Non-JD ordering (/non-jd). Shows how many parts need ordering at your branch and what they would cost, with a **Review now** link straight to that list, plus a card for each page. Pick a different branch in the greeting line to look at another branch.' },
    { p: 'The count leaves out lines that are only there because of an Equip minimum on a part with no demand (see "Min, no demand" below). A small line says how many were left out, for example "(21 more with minimums but no demand)".' },
    { p: 'Below it, a **purple transfers card** sums up this morning’s transfer suggestions, for example "Branch 1: 12 parts could come by transfer instead of being bought", with the totals for every branch underneath. **Review transfers** opens Recommended Reorder on **step 1 (Transfers)** for your branch, where you tick and save them. The card is hidden on a morning with no suggestions.' },

    { h2: 'Recommended Reorder' },
    { p: 'The main page: every part at the branch with the suggested order quantity.' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Branch and Franchise** pickers at the top. The page remembers them in the address, so a bookmark or a refresh keeps your view. **Several branches…** shows more than one branch at once (below).',
      '**Dealer group:** tick one or more dealer groups to list only their parts. None ticked means every group; **(none)** is the parts with no dealer group code.',
      '**Reorder needed only** (on by default) shows just the parts to order (Out of stock and Reorder). Turn it off to see every part.',
      '**Show minimums with no demand** (off by default): see "Min, no demand" below.',
      '**Transfer suggested only** lists every part another branch could send (below), ordered or not, so redistribution-only parts with an order of 0 show too. While it is on, **Reorder needed only** is greyed out. It only shows when there are suggestions.',
      '**Leaving with ticked parts:** if you go to another page while parts are ticked, the app asks first ("Leave this page? 2 ticked parts will be lost."). **Stay** keeps them. One-Time Order asks the same on its Transfers and review steps.',
      '**Search** by part number or description.',
      '**Sort** by clicking a column heading; click again to reverse.',
      'Click the **arrow** at the left of a row to see **why** that quantity was suggested, in plain English. It also shows any suggested transfers, the rule used, inbound transfers, other branches with stock above their target, and the **Part actions** (below).',
      '**Inbound** counts transfers on their way: the ones open in Equip plus the ones saved in this app (a small **app** tag; hover over the number: “Equip transfers + transfers saved in this app”, with the split).',
      'Click a **part number** to open its Part page.',
    ] },
    { h3: 'Min, no demand' },
    { p: 'An Equip minimum always applies, even on a part nobody has asked for in the rule’s history months. Those lines carry a **Min, no demand** tag. They are hidden until you turn on **Show minimums with no demand**, and even then they are only ordered if you tick them. Many of these minimums are old or mistyped in Equip, so check them before ordering.' },
    { p: 'Example: Branch 1, part **408DU** (4x8 cross hydraulic cylinder). No demand in the last 12 months, Equip minimum 2, none on hand, cost $245.02. The tool suggests 2 ($490.04), tagged Min, no demand, and hidden until you turn the switch on.' },
    { h3: 'Transfers on Recommended Reorder' },
    { p: 'Parts another branch could send are marked in **purple**, so they stand out while you scan the list:' },
    { bullets: [
      'A purple banner above the list: for example "3 parts here could come by transfer instead of being bought", with **See Transfers**.',
      'Each such row has a purple edge and a pill before the order: **Transfer 1 from 92**, or **Transfer 3 from 2 branches** when several branches could send. **Redistribution 9 from 92** means extra stock is on offer, not a replacement for buying (section 5).',
      'In the row detail, a purple **Suggested transfers** box comes first: for example "Branch 92 can send 1 instead of buying (Conventional), and 9 more (Redistribution)."',
    ] },
    { p: 'A suggestion alone doesn’t lower the order: the order counts **saved** transfers only. Save them on step 1 (below).' },
    { h3: 'Saving transfers: step 1 and step 2' },
    { p: 'When any transfer is suggested into the branches you picked, Recommended Reorder opens on **step 1** of **1 Transfers → 2 Order**; otherwise it opens on step 2 and step 1 says “No transfers suggested”.' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Step 1** lists every suggestion into your branches, **Conventional** and **Redistribution**, all ticked to start with, with a franchise filter. Untick what you don’t want, or change a quantity (a whole number, never more than suggested: “At most 44”). The footer under the list says what the ticked lines do, for example “2 transfers cover 2 order lines and $38.00 you won’t buy.” It stays at the bottom of the window while you scroll, with its own **Save** button.',
      '**Search:** the part search box at the top of the page also filters step 1’s list, by part number or description. It doesn’t change what is ticked, and Save still saves every ticked line. When the search hides ticked lines, the footer says so: “3 of 10 ticked lines shown”.',
      'Every branch loads in the background first. Until then the list is this morning’s, marked “as of this morning”, and can’t be saved (“Loading every branch… you can save once it finishes”), so a save is always checked against live stock.',
      '**Save N transfers (N transfer orders)** saves the ticked lines as one **transfer order** per sending and receiving branch, all franchises together, and lists them under **Saved transfers** with a link to each.',
      'Just before saving, the app checks each sending branch still has the stock. If someone else took it meanwhile, **nothing is saved**: a banner lists what changed (for example “… from 92 to 1: 9 → 4 (branch 92 now has only 4 to send)” or “no longer suggested”), the ticks are adjusted, and you click Save again.',
      'If SharePoint is slow to show what you saved: “Saved. The list hasn’t caught up yet — Refresh in a moment.” The order already counts it. **Refresh** re-reads the saved transfers at any time.',
      '**Continue to order ›** goes to step 2. With unsaved ticks it asks “Save the N ticked transfers first?”: **Save and continue** or **Continue without them**. The order only ever counts saved transfers. If someone else used the stock after you ticked, nothing is saved: a banner lists what changed, with “Check the changes below and save again.”',
      '**Step 2** is the order list. A line a saved transfer reduced says **Includes 44 coming by transfer from branch 6**: that quantity is part of Inbound, not extra; a line it covered fully has nothing left to order and leaves “Reorder needed only”.',
      'Leaving the page with unsaved transfer changes or ticked parts asks first.',
    ] },
    { h3: 'Several branches at once' },
    { p: 'Click **Several branches…** and tick the branches you want (or **Select all**). Each loads by itself, with a progress line. The list then has a **Branch** column, a **Totals by branch** table, and a **Show** picker to look at one branch at a time. Search, ticks and sorting work across every loaded branch. **Create orders** makes one order per **branch and franchise**. Unticking a branch unticks its parts, and says how many. **Clear** and tick one branch to go back to the single-branch view.' },
    { h3: 'Part actions: Mask, Manual order and Phase in' },
    { p: 'In the row detail, **Mask…** stops the tool suggesting the part, with an optional expiry date. **Manual order** stops it until someone turns it off. **Phase in…** sets a phase-in (see Part settings). On a part that is already masked or on Manual order, the buttons are **Unmask** and **Clear manual**. Each one asks before saving, and the row updates straight away. The same buttons are on each line of the order review, and masking a line there takes it out of the order. A part with a phase-in has a small green **Phase-in** tag by its part number (amber **Phase-in ended** once it has ended).' },
    { h3: 'Making an order from the suggestions' },
    { steps: [
      'Tick the parts you want (the box in the heading ticks every part listed by the current filter, on every page).',
      'The bar at the bottom shows how many are ticked and their value at cost. Ticked parts stay ticked when you search or sort. Changing the branch or franchise asks first, because it clears them.',
      'Click **Create order**. A review lists one order per franchise and every ticked part. Parts hidden by your current search are marked "Hidden by filter", so nothing is ordered by surprise.',
      'Change any quantity. A quantity of 0 leaves the line out.',
      'Save. The tool creates the order(s) and opens them.',
    ], ref: 'steps' },

    { h2: 'Part Lookup and the Part page' },
    { p: 'Search one branch, or **All branches** to find a part anywhere. The Part page has three tabs:' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Detail:** stock, prices, the suggested order and "Why this number", plus **Other branches** (below).',
      '**History:** a chart of the last 12 full months (switch between units sold and requests), a 60-month table and totals.',
      '**Settings:** this part’s own minimum, Manual order, a mask, a phase-in and notes (below). Saving applies straight away for everyone.',
    ] },
    { h3: 'Other branches' },
    { p: 'Every branch loads by itself when the page opens. This branch is listed first, marked "(this branch)". The columns are **On hand**, **Target**, **Above target**, **Excess**, **Months of supply** and **Sold 12 mo**, and a **Total** row at the bottom adds up every branch, this one included. While branches are still loading, the totals end with "+". Hover over a column heading for its meaning.' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Above target** = on hand above that branch’s target (round 1 called this "Excess"). Example: 10 on hand and a target of 4 is 6 above target.',
      '**Excess** = stock that branch could **send**: free stock above what it keeps, which is the larger of its target and 12 months of supply. Stock committed to open tickets doesn’t count. This is John Deere’s meaning of excess (section 5).',
      '**Months of supply** = on hand ÷ the monthly rate. "—" means the part has no sales there.',
    ] },
    { p: 'Above the table, **Suggested transfers** lists this part’s transfers into or out of this branch once every branch has loaded, for example "Suggested: 4 from branch 92 (conventional)".' },
    { h3: 'Part settings' },
    { table: [
      ['Setting', 'What it does'],
      ['Min override', 'This part’s own minimum. The target is never below it, and it replaces the Equip minimum. Whole numbers only; blank means none. A min override of 0 switches off an Equip minimum you don’t trust.'],
      ['Manual order', 'The tool never suggests this part until someone turns it off. For parts you always order by hand.'],
      ['Masked', 'Stops the tool suggesting this part (order 0). The mask ends at the **first** of: the end of the expiry date, if you give one; or the next time the part is asked for (a demand after the day it was masked). A mask with no expiry date lasts until the part is asked for again or someone unmasks it.'],
      ['Phase-in', 'Stocks a new part before it has the demands its rule needs. Give a **quantity** and an **end date** (it fills in 12 months from today; blank means no end date; it can’t be in the past). While it is active, the target is at least that quantity. It ends at the end date (it still applies on its last day), or as soon as the part has the demands its rule needs, because the rule then stocks it on its own. A badge next to the heading says **Active until …**, **Ended …** or **Ignored** (a quantity that isn’t a whole number from 1 to 99,999). **Clear phase-in** removes it. Masked and Manual order still win.'],
      ['Notes', 'Free text for anyone looking at the part.'],
      ['EOQ, Force non-spiking, Pre-approved rule', 'Saved, but not used by the calculation yet (planned for when John Deere parts join).'],
    ], widths: w(0.24, 0.76) },
    { p: 'Example of a mask ending: a part masked on October 5 with no expiry is asked for on October 9. From the next morning’s snapshot the mask has ended and the part is suggested again if it needs ordering. Its Settings tab then reads "the mask ended when the part was requested on 2026-10-09".' },

    { h2: 'Special statuses' },
    { p: 'One list of every part with a special setting: **Masked**, **Manual order**, a **Min override**, a **Phase-in**, or a **mask or phase-in that has ended** (so someone can tidy it). It shows the branch, part, description, on hand, status, who set it and when, the expiry or phase-in and the notes. Filter by status and branch, click a part to open it, and **Export CSV** for a spreadsheet.' },

    { h2: 'Transfers' },
    { p: 'Saved transfers and every suggested transfer between branches, all branches and franchises together, in two tabs: **Transfer orders** (first) and **Suggestions**. **Transfers** in the menu opens on **Transfer orders** (below). Home’s **Review transfers** and the links on Recommended Reorder (**See Transfers**) go to the suggestions.' },
    { p: 'The **Suggestions** tab:' },
    { bullets: [
      'It starts with this morning’s suggestions (**As of this morning**) and loads every branch in the background, with a progress line. Once all have loaded it switches to **Live**: worked out again with the current rules, part settings and saved transfers.',
      'A banner then says whether anything changed since this morning, for example "1 part has changed since this morning", and **Why** (a rule or a part setting changed). A red banner saying the app disagrees with this morning’s run is a bug: tell Brian.',
      '**Filters** at the top: **Franchise**, **Kind** (Conventional or Redistribution), **From**, **To** and **Status**: **Suggested**, or **Saved today** (today’s saved transfer lines; cancelled transfers aren’t listed there: see **Transfer orders** with Status **Cancelled**). Statuses read **Entered in Equip** for a transfer keyed into Equip.',
      '**Tick and save** as on Reorder step 1. Save takes only the ticked lines **you can see** with the current filters, so you can work one sending branch at a time. When ticked lines are further down: “N more ticked lines below — Show more to include them”.',
      'Each line has a **kind badge**: **Conventional** (another branch sends what this branch would otherwise buy) or **Redistribution** (extra stock to a branch that sells the part and has room).',
      'The columns show the receiving branch’s need, the quantity, and the sending branch’s on hand, months of supply and excess as "before → after" this transfer, plus the receiving branch’s months of supply before → after and the value.',
      'The line under the table totals each kind. **Export CSV** downloads the filtered list: `Kind, FromBranch, ToBranch, PartNumber, Franchise, Description, Qty, FromBin, ToBin, FromCost, Value`.',
      '**Transfer rules in use** (at the bottom, closed to start with) lists the transfer rules, or the built-in default, with a link to the Transfer rules page.',
    ] },
    { h3: 'Transfer orders' },
    { p: 'The **Transfer orders** tab, where the page opens, lists every saved transfer, newest first: From, To, status, lines, value at the sending branch’s cost, export count and who saved it when. Filter by From, To and status. A Draft more than a week old shows **Draft for N days**, and the tab label counts them, so nothing is forgotten.' },
    { p: 'Each status is a coloured pill: **Draft** amber, **Exported** blue, **Entered in Equip** green, **Cancelled** grey.' },
    { p: 'Open one to see its lines. A transfer order goes **Draft → Exported → Entered in Equip**:' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Draft:** change a quantity (never above the suggested quantity; 0 means “Not sent”; lines are never deleted).',
      '**Export CSV** downloads `PartNumber, Franchise, Description, Qty, FromBin, ToBin, Cost, LineValue` for keying into Equip, and marks it Exported. After that its quantities can’t change; start a new transfer instead.',
      '**Mark entered in Equip** once it is keyed in. It needs an export first, because the CSV is the record of what was keyed.',
      '**Cancel** (Draft or Exported only) releases the stock for other suggestions. Nothing is deleted.',
      '**Archive** hides an entered or cancelled transfer from the list (**Show archived** brings it back). An archived transfer is read-only until unarchived.',
      'If someone else exported, entered, cancelled or archived it while you had it open, your change is refused with a message saying so, and the page shows the current state.',
    ] },
    { p: '**Export several at once (0.13.1).** Tick transfer orders on the list (the box in the heading ticks every one the filters show), then click **Export (N)**. One file downloads, `Transfer-orders-<date>.csv`: `Transfer, From, To, PartNumber, Franchise, Description, Qty, FromBin, ToBin, Cost, LineValue`, newest transfer first, each with its lines. Every transfer in the file counts as exported, exactly as if you had exported it on its own (Exported, export count + 1).' },
    { bullets: [
      'Only **Draft** or **Exported** transfers that aren’t archived can be exported; with any other ticked, the button is greyed out and says so.',
      'Each transfer is read again first. One that someone entered, cancelled or archived meanwhile, or that has no line with a quantity, is left out, and the message names it, for example “Exported 5 transfer orders (42 lines). Left out: 6-2-… (no line with a quantity).”',
      'If some couldn’t be recorded as exported (for example the sign-in expired), a box lists them: **Try again** records them without downloading again, **Dismiss** closes it. **Export** waits until the box is cleared, so none is forgotten.',
    ] },

    { h2: 'Orders' },
    { p: 'Every saved order, newest first, with filters for branch and status. An order is for **one branch and one franchise**.' },
    { table: [
      ['Status', 'What you can do'],
      ['Draft', 'Change quantities (press Enter or click away to save each one) and remove lines. Export when ready.'],
      ['Exported', '**Export CSV** downloads the file for the vendor and locks the lines, because the file has gone out. You can export it again (the count goes up).'],
      ['Placed', '**Mark placed** once the order is with the vendor. The order is then read-only.'],
    ], widths: w(0.18, 0.82) },
    { p: 'The CSV file has one line per part: `PartNumber, Description, OrderQty, Cost, LineValue, Bin`. Description and bin are saved with the order, so a file exported later still matches what was ordered. Tip: open it in Notepad to see it exactly; Excel drops leading zeros from part numbers on screen.' },
    { p: '**Export together (0.5.1).** Tick several orders and a panel offers one CSV instead of one per order (only Draft and Exported orders go in; placed and archived ones are left out, and the panel says how many it takes). After you create orders for several branches, the new orders open already ticked, with **Download one CSV for all**. Two choices, remembered in your browser:' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Lines:** **One line per branch** (a Branch column first; every order line as it is) or **Total per part** (one line per franchise and part number, quantities and values added up, with a **Branches** column such as “1 (4); 92 (2)”). When the branches’ costs differ, Cost is the quantity-weighted average. A part number in two franchises stays two lines.',
      '**Files:** **One file per franchise** (each goes to its own vendor) or **One file for everything** (a Franchise column).',
    ] },
    { p: 'Every order in the file counts as exported, exactly as if you had exported it on its own (Exported, export count + 1). The orders themselves stay saved one per branch and franchise.' },
    { p: '**Archiving orders (0.5.2).** Tick orders and click **Archive (N)**, or use **Archive** on an order’s own page, to hide orders you no longer need to see (test orders, orders placed long ago). Nothing is deleted and nothing else changes: status, export count and lines stay as they were. Archived orders are hidden unless **Show archived** is on (remembered on your computer), where they show greyed with an **Archived** tag. An archived order is read-only until you click **Unarchive**.' },

    { h2: 'One-Time Order' },
    { p: 'For a one-off order based on sales over months you choose, e.g. stocking up before a season.' },
    { steps: [
      '**Set up order:** name the order, tick one or more **branches** and **franchises**, optionally tick **dealer groups** (none ticked = every group), and choose the months: Rolling 12/24/36, a full calendar year, or tick any months. Click a highlighted range again to clear it. Optionally set a **Min line value** and **Max line value** in dollars (section 6). One order is made for each branch and franchise that has parts to order. Click **Continue to transfers**.',
      '**Transfers** (0.7.0): every branch loads first (a few seconds the first time), then the page lists stock other branches can spare for this order’s lines (below). Tick and save them, then click **Continue to review ›**. When nothing can come by transfer, this step is skipped and Review says “No transfers suggested for this order.”',
      '**Review & calculate:** choose **Total** or **Avg / mo**, the factor (called **Months of supply** with Avg / mo), and optional minimum sales / minimum demands. The list updates as you change them. Untick anything you don’t want.',
      '**Create order:** the same review and save as above; the order is marked One-Time.',
    ], ref: 'steps2' },
    { p: 'Masked and Manual order parts are left off One-Time Orders completely. The review says how many were left off. A phase-in doesn’t affect One-Time Orders.' },
    { p: '**Templates** (0.10.0): the **Template** card at the top of Set up fills in a saved set-up with one click, or saves this one for next time. Section 9 explains it.' },
    { h3: 'The Transfers step' },
    { p: 'It works like Recommended Reorder step 1: “Transfers into branch 1”, every line ticked to start with, a quantity never above the suggestion, a **Search parts** box, and a footer with its own **Save**. The **Order** column shows the line’s one-time quantity before → after the ticked transfer, for example **22 → 0**. What it suggests:' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Conventional only:** transfers instead of buying. No redistribution: a one-time buy is a single purchase.',
      'The stock comes from **other branches’ excess**, and **each sending branch’s own transfer rule** decides what it can spare (section 5), exactly as for the daily suggestions.',
      'A branch that is **buying the part in this order never sends it**. It can still send a part it isn’t buying here.',
      'A branch whose transfer rule doesn’t allow receiving gets no transfers.',
      '**This morning’s daily suggestions aren’t held back** for the daily order. Only **saved** transfers count, as everywhere else.',
    ] },
    { p: '**Continue to review ›** with unsaved ticks asks “Save the N ticked transfers first?” (**Stay**, **Continue without them**, **Save and continue**). Review counts **saved** transfers only. They come off the quantity: a **Transfer in** column shows them, with “Includes 12 coming by transfer from branch 92” on the line. A line saved transfers cover fully stays on Review with **0** and **Covered by transfers**: it can’t be ticked, isn’t in the totals and is never ordered.' },
    { p: 'Changing the order’s settings after editing the ticks asks first (“Your transfer changes will be lost.”), because the transfers are worked out again. A transfer order saved here says **From One-Time Order "<name>"** on its page.' },

    { h2: 'Templates' },
    { p: 'In the menu right under One-Time Order (0.10.0): every saved One-Time template, with **Start order**, **History** and **Archive**. Section 9 explains it.' },

    { h2: 'Rules' },
    { p: 'The rules that drive every suggestion (how much history to look at, how much to stock). Anyone in the test group can change them, and the page shows who last changed each rule and when. Section 4 explains them.' },
    { p: '**Try it** lets you test a change before saving it: click **Edit** on a rule, change a number, and the panel shows a branch before and after: order lines, total value, status counts and the parts that change most. Nothing is saved until you click **Save rule**. If you leave with unsaved changes, it asks first.' },
    { p: '**Archive** hides a rule you no longer use, instead of deleting it. Archived rules are never used. They are hidden unless **Show archived** is on; then they show greyed out, and **Unarchive** brings one back.' },

    { h2: 'Transfer rules' },
    { p: 'The rules that decide how much a branch keeps before stock counts as excess, whether it may send or receive, and how much redistribution it takes (section 5). It works like Rules: a list with each rule’s state (**Active**, **Off**, **Out of season**, **Archived**, or **Invalid** with the reason, which means it is skipped), an editor, **Archive** instead of delete, and **Try it**.' },
    { bullets: [
      'The first row is the read-only **Built-in default**: excess above 12 months, receive up to 6 months, no minimum for redistribution. It applies wherever no rule matches.',
      '**Try it** shows, while you edit, the effect on every branch before you save: conventional lines and **Order $ avoided**, redistribution lines and dollars, before → after, and the parts that change most. Nothing is saved until you click Save. A rule with a problem shows the problem instead.',
      'Saving applies straight away in the app; the morning run uses it the next day. Switching on a rule that has problems asks first, because it would be skipped.',
    ] },

    { h2: 'Cleanup' },
    { p: 'Its own area (0.11.0): open it from the launch page or the Area select; its menu item is **Checks**. **Cleanup checks** lists checks, each a list of parts to fix in Equip over every branch: three built in (**Minimums with no demand**, as before, **Stock value with no quantity** and **Stock on superseded parts**) and any that people save. Open one to filter it by branch and franchise, sort it and export a CSV; **New check** builds your own. Section 7 explains it job by job.' },

    { h2: 'Returns & scrap' },
    { p: 'Its own area (0.9.0): **Returns**, **Scrap** and **Return & scrap rules**. Section 8 explains it job by job.' },

    { h2: 'Inventory management' },
    { p: 'Its own area (0.12.0): open it from the launch page or the Area select; its menu item is **Reports**. **Inventory reports** lists the Corp Parts reports over the JD parts at every branch: the built-ins **MLPF Gaps**, **Border Stores**, **2 & 4 Cotton**, **Corporate Stocking** and **Stock on superseded parts**, and any copies people save. Open one to filter it, search it, sort it and export a CSV; **Copy as my own** makes a version you can change. Sections 10 to 13 explain it job by job.' },

    { h2: 'App Settings' },
    { p: 'Your own default branch and franchise (saved in your browser only). The launch page, Reorder, One-Time Order and Part Lookup open on them. Open it from the bottom of the left menu or from the launch page. **About** shows the version.' },

    { h1: '3. How the suggested order is worked out' },
    { p: 'For each part at each branch, every morning and again in the tool whenever you change a rule or setting:' },
    { steps: [
      '**Pick the rule** that applies to the part (section 4).',
      '**Count demands** over the rule’s **history months**. A demand is one transaction (one request for the part), however many units it was for. Full months only; the current month isn’t finished, so it’s left out.',
      '**Add up units sold** over the same months (or the number of requests, if the rule says so). Returns are netted off, and a total below 0 counts as 0.',
      '**Monthly rate** = units sold ÷ history months. A part that sold 12 in 12 months has a rate of 1 a month, whether the sales came in one month or twelve.',
      '**Supply in months.** The rule’s supply is in weeks or months. Weeks are turned into months: **weeks × 12 ÷ 52**. So 12 weeks = 2.77 months.',
      '**Rule target.** If the part had at least the rule’s **min demands**, the rule target is **monthly rate × months of supply, rounded up**. Otherwise the rule target is 0 (the rule doesn’t stock it).',
      '**Minimum.** The part’s own **min override** (Part page) applies if set. If not, the **Equip minimum** applies, always, even with no demand.',
      '**Phase-in.** A phase-in is **active** while it has a quantity, its end date hasn’t passed, and the part has fewer demands than its rule needs (at least 1). It is checked fresh every morning.',
      '**Target** = the largest of the rule target, the minimum (rounded up) and an active phase-in quantity.',
      '**Order** = target − on hand − on order − **transfers in** (transfers saved in this app), never below 0. On hand and on order count whole units. **A target of 0 always means order 0**, even when Equip shows negative stock.',
      '**Line value limits** (only if the rule has them): if the line is worth more than the **max line value**, the quantity is cut back to fit under it. Then, if the line is worth less than the **min line value**, nothing is suggested.',
      '**Manual order or masked:** the order is 0, even with a phase-in.',
    ], ref: 'steps3' },

    { h3: 'Worked example' },
    { p: 'Branch 1, part **124184** (Mobil 1 0W20 motor oil), Default rule (12 months, 3 demands, 12 weeks of supply). Dev data of October 26, 2026; the 12 months are October 2025 to September 2026.' },
    { table: [
      ['Step', 'Working', 'Result'],
      ['Demands', '22 transactions in the 12 months. 22 is at least 3, so the rule stocks it', 'stocked'],
      ['Units sold', '170 sold in the 12 months', '170'],
      ['Monthly rate', '170 ÷ 12', '14.17 a month'],
      ['Supply in months', '12 weeks × 12 ÷ 52', '2.77 months'],
      ['Rule target', '14.17 × 2.77 = 39.2, rounded up', '40'],
      ['Minimum', 'none set', '–'],
      ['Order', '40 target − 1 on hand − 0 on order', '**39** ($464.10 at $11.90)'],
    ], widths: w(0.2, 0.55, 0.25), keep: true },
    { p: 'A shortcut that gives the same answer with 12 months of history: units sold in the year × weeks ÷ 52 = 170 × 12 ÷ 52 = 39.2, rounded up to 40.' },
    { p: '**The same part with supply in months.** If the rule said **3 months** of supply instead: 14.17 × 3 = 42.5, rounded up to a target of 43, so the order is 43 − 1 = **42**.' },
    { p: '**Line value limits on the same part** (a rule with a max line value of $300 and a min line value of $50):', keepNext: true },
    { bullets: [
      'The order of 39 is worth 39 × $11.90 = $464.10, more than $300. The quantity is cut back to what $300 buys: $300 ÷ $11.90 = 25.2, rounded down to **25** ($297.50). That is still above the $50 minimum, so 25 is suggested, with a **Max value** badge.',
      'If there were 37 on hand, the line would be 40 − 37 = 3, worth $35.70. That is under the $50 minimum, so nothing is suggested (status **Below min value**).',
      'The cap is applied first, then the minimum.',
    ] },
    { p: '**Too few demands.** Branch 1, part **1426W** (26" wide neck row crop sweep) sold 30 units, but in only 2 transactions. 2 is fewer than 3, so the rule doesn’t stock it (**Not stocked**).' },
    { p: '**A minimum at work.** Branch 91, the same 1426W: 39 sold in 3 demands, so the rule target is 3.25 a month × 2.77 = 9. The Equip minimum is 12, so the target is 12. With 2 on hand the order is **10**. And at Branch 1, part **09-1785273** sold 4 in 1 demand, so the rule alone doesn’t stock it; a min override of 5 on its Part page makes the target 5 and, with none on hand, the order 5.' },

    { h3: 'Status' },
    { p: 'Each part gets the first status in this list that fits it:', keepNext: true },
    { table: [
      ['Status', 'Meaning'],
      ['Manual order', 'Someone set Manual order on the part; it is never suggested until that is turned off.'],
      ['Masked', 'Someone masked it, and the mask hasn’t ended; no order.'],
      ['No recent activity', 'The target is 0 and the part had no demands and no units sold in the history months.'],
      ['Not stocked', 'The part had some activity, but the target came out 0 (usually fewer demands than the rule needs) and there is no minimum.'],
      ['Below min value', 'An order was due, but the line is worth less than the rule’s min line value, so nothing is suggested.'],
      ['Above max value', 'An order was due, but one unit costs more than the rule’s max line value, so nothing is suggested.'],
      ['Out of stock', 'An order is suggested and there is none on hand.'],
      ['Reorder', 'An order is suggested; some stock is on hand.'],
      ['Stocked', 'The part is stocked and there’s enough; no order needed.'],
    ], widths: w(0.22, 0.78), keep: true },
    { p: 'Two badges can appear next to the status: **Max value** (the quantity was cut back to fit the max line value) and **Min value** (the line was under the min line value). The **Min, no demand** tag marks a line that is there only because of an Equip minimum on a part with no activity.' },
    { h3: 'Other numbers you’ll see' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Above target** = on hand above that branch’s target. **Excess** = stock a branch could send (section 5). **Months of supply** = on hand ÷ monthly rate.',
      '**Inbound** = transfers on their way to this branch. Transfers open in **Equip** are already in Equip’s on order, so they don’t reduce the order a second time. Transfers **saved in this app** (tagged **app**) do reduce it.',
      '**Est. value** = suggested order × cost. Example: 39 × $11.90 = $464.10.',
      '**"Changed"** marks a part whose number differs from this morning’s because someone changed a rule or a part setting since.',
    ] },

    { h1: '4. Rules' },
    { p: 'Each rule says how to work out the target for the parts it covers. A rule can be narrowed by **franchise**, **dealer group code**, **branch**, **SLC**, **source**, **vendor code**, **product group** and **commodity code** (blank means any; case and spaces at either end don’t matter), and limited to a **season** (start and end month; 11 to 2 means November to February). Only **active** rules that aren’t **archived** are used.' },
    { p: 'Each branch’s own part record decides, so a part can match at one branch and not another. **Vendor code** is the whole number without leading zeros (1234, not 01234); 0 means parts with no vendor code. **Commodity code** is empty for most non-JD parts today.' },
    { h3: 'Which rule a part uses' },
    { steps: [
      'Of the active, in-season rules that fit the part, the **highest priority** wins.',
      'On a tie, the **more specific** rule wins (more of the eight criteria filled in).',
      'Then the lowest rule number.',
      'If none fits, the built-in default applies: 12 months, 3 demands, 12 weeks of supply, units sold, no line value limits.',
    ], ref: 'steps4' },
    { table: [
      ['Setting', 'What it does', 'Higher means…', 'Lower means…'],
      ['History months (1–59)', 'How far back demand is measured', 'Smoother; slow parts get more history', 'Reacts faster to recent changes'],
      ['Min demands (0–9999)', 'Demands (transactions) in the history months needed before the rule stocks a part', 'Fewer, steadier parts stocked', 'More occasional sellers stocked'],
      ['Supply and Supply unit', 'How much stock to hold: 0–104 weeks or 0–24 months', 'More stock (long lead times, busy season)', 'Less stock (quick resupply, off season)'],
      ['Min line value ($, optional)', 'A line worth less than this isn’t suggested', 'Fewer small lines', 'More small lines'],
      ['Max line value ($, optional)', 'A line is cut back to stay at or under this', 'Bigger lines allowed', 'Lines cut back sooner'],
      ['Demand basis', '**UnitsSold** counts quantities; **Requests** counts how often it was asked for', 'Use Requests when a few big sales would overstate demand', ''],
    ], widths: w(0.22, 0.3, 0.24, 0.24), keep: true },
    { p: '**Example: cotton.** Two example rules exist for dealer group COTTON, switched off: "Cotton - in season" (June–October, 10 weeks of supply, 2 demands) and "Cotton - off season" (November–May, 3 weeks of supply, 3 demands). Both have priority 10, so they beat Default for cotton parts. Try them in **Try it** before switching them on. Dealer group codes are set per branch in Equip, so a part may be COTTON at one branch and not another.' },
    { p: 'A rule with a missing or out-of-range setting is shown as **Skipped** with the reason, and isn’t used. A rule that is active but out of season shows **Not used this month**.' },

    { h1: '5. Transfers: how the suggestions are worked out' },
    { p: 'Every morning, and live on the Transfers page, each part is worked out on its own across all branches:' },
    { steps: [
      '**What a branch keeps** = the larger of its target and its **excess supply** (12 months of supply by default, rounded up).',
      '**Excess** = on hand − stock committed to open tickets − transfers out saved in this app − what it keeps, never below 0. A part with no sales has everything above its target as excess.',
      '**Conventional (instead of buying):** a branch whose line would be ordered, and that has no excess of its own, can receive its **need** = target − on hand − on order − transfers in. A **Min, no demand** line never pulls a transfer, unless the part has an active phase-in.',
      '**Who goes first:** the branch with the **lowest months of supply receives first**. It takes from the branch with the **greatest months of supply** first, choosing again after every line, until its need is filled or no excess is left.',
      '**Redistribution:** leftover excess can go to a branch that already stocks and sells the part, up to **6 months of supply** (never above what it would itself keep), in the same order. There is no minimum line value by default.',
      '**Value** = quantity × the receiving branch’s cost. The sending branch’s own order never changes.',
    ], ref: 'steps6' },
    { p: '**Example** (round numbers). Branch 92 has 40 of a chain, sells 1 a month and its target is 2, so it keeps 12 (12 months of supply) and has 40 − 12 = **28** excess. Branch 1 sells 2 a month, has none on hand and a target of 3. Branch 1 has the lowest months of supply, so it receives first: **Transfer 3 from 92** (conventional), leaving 92 with 25 excess. Branch 1 can then take up to 6 months of supply = 12 in all, so 9 more come as **redistribution**. If 2 of the 40 were already on a saved transfer to branch 1, 92’s excess would be 26 and the conventional transfer 1.' },
    { h3: 'Transfer rules' },
    { p: 'Transfer rules use the same criteria, priority and seasons as order rules. **The sending branch’s rule** sets its excess supply (**ExcessSupply**, weeks or months), whether it may send (**AllowSend**), and a minimum line value for redistribution (**RedistMinValue**). **The receiving branch’s rule** sets whether it may receive (**AllowReceive**) and its redistribution maximum (**RedistMax**; 0 = no redistribution into that branch). With no rule, the built-in default applies: 12 months, both allowed, 6 months, no minimum.' },
    { p: 'Transfer rules are edited on the **Transfer rules** page (section 2). A rule with a missing or out-of-range setting is skipped: the page shows it as **Invalid** with the reason, and Brian’s morning email names it.' },
    { h3: 'Transfers on a One-Time Order (0.7.0)' },
    { p: 'The One-Time Order’s Transfers step runs only the **conventional** half of the above. The receivers are the order’s lines at the ticked branches, and each one’s **need** is its one-time quantity before the line value limits, less transfers already saved into it. The senders are every other branch’s **excess**, worked out with that branch’s own transfer rule. Then, as above, the lowest months of supply receives first and takes from the greatest months of supply first. A branch buying the part in this order never sends it, a branch that doesn’t allow receiving gets nothing, and this morning’s daily suggestions aren’t subtracted from the senders.' },
    { h3: 'Saved transfers' },
    { p: 'A saved transfer counts as stock on its way: it lowers the receiving branch’s order (transfers in) and the sending branch’s excess (transfers out), straight away in the app and in the next morning’s run. A **Draft** or **Exported** transfer counts until it is entered or cancelled. One **entered in Equip** counts until the next morning’s run, when Equip itself shows it. A **cancelled** transfer never counts. Archiving changes nothing.' },

    { h1: '6. One-Time Order: the calculation' },
    { steps: [
      '**Units sold** are added up over the months you picked (a net total below 0 counts as 0).',
      '**Total:** anticipated = units sold × loading factor. **Avg / mo:** anticipated = (units sold ÷ number of months) × **months of supply**.',
      '**Order** = anticipated, rounded up, − on hand − on order − **transfers in** (transfers saved in this app), never below 0. A line the saved transfers cover fully shows **Covered by transfers** and is never ordered.',
      '**Line value limits**, if you set them: a line worth more than the max is cut back to fit under it, then a line worth less than the min is dropped. The same as in a rule.',
      'Parts below **minimum sales** (units sold) or **minimum demands** (requests), both over the chosen months, are left off. 0 means no filter.',
      'Only parts with an order above 0 are listed. Masked and Manual order parts are never listed.',
    ], ref: 'steps5' },
    { table: [
      ['Example', 'Working', 'Order'],
      ['Total, factor 1.5', '20 sold in 12 months × 1.5 = 30; 30 − 10 on hand', '**20**'],
      ['Avg / mo, 3 months of supply', '24 sold in 12 months = 2 a month × 3 = 6; 6 − 1 on hand', '**5**'],
      ['Total, factor 1.5, max line value $100, cost $8', 'The order of 20 is worth $160, over $100. $100 ÷ $8 = 12.5, rounded down', '**12** ($96)'],
    ], widths: w(0.28, 0.54, 0.18), keep: true },

    { h1: '7. Cleanup checks (0.11.0)' },
    { p: 'The **Cleanup** area lists things to fix in Equip. Since 0.11.0 it is a list of **checks**: each check is a list of parts, over every branch, that meet its conditions. Two are built in, and anyone can build more without waiting for a new version of the app.' },
    { note: 'Nothing here changes Equip. A check is a list you filter, export and work through; the fixes are made in Equip as today. A fixed part leaves the list with the next morning’s data.' },

    { h2: 'The list of checks' },
    { p: 'Open **Cleanup** from the launch page (or the Area select); **Checks** in the menu. The page is **Cleanup checks**: “Lists of parts to fix in Equip, over every branch. Open one to filter it and export a CSV.” It loads every branch first, with a progress bar. Each row shows:' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Check:** the name (click it to open the check), the description, and its conditions in one line, for example “On hand > 0 and Sales 24 months = 0 and Cost > $100.00”.',
      '**By:** **Built in**, or who made the check and who changed it last, for example “Ben Hill · changed Nov 2, 3:40 PM by Shannon Cole”.',
      '**Parts:** how many parts it finds over every branch. The counts fill in as the branches load (“in 12 of 19 branches” until they all have). The counts change every morning.',
      'The buttons: **Open** for every check; **Edit**, **Switch off** / **Switch on** and **Archive** for saved checks.',
    ] },
    { p: 'The three built-in checks come first, then the saved checks that are on (A to Z), then any that are switched off. On November 2, 2026 (Prod): **Minimums with no demand** found **3,592** parts and **Stock value with no quantity** **415**. **Stock on superseded parts** (0.15.0, section 13) found **698** on November 5.' },
    { p: 'The launch page card still shows only the minimums with no demand at your branch, so it is a much smaller number than the list’s count over every branch.' },

    { h2: 'Minimums with no demand' },
    { p: 'The same list as before 0.11.0, now opened from the checks list. Its page has **← Cleanup checks** at the top to go back.' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Listed:** parts with an Equip minimum above 0, no min override, and **no demands and no units sold** in the rule’s history months, as of this morning’s snapshot: the lines that carry the **Min, no demand** tag on Recommended Reorder. These minimums **do** apply to the order, so this is the list to fix.',
      '**Min × cost** = Equip minimum × cost: the stock value that minimum commits. Example: 408DU, minimum 2 × $245.02 = $490.04.',
      '**Would order $** = Equip minimum (rounded up) − on hand − on order, never below 0, × cost: what the minimum makes the tool suggest today.',
      '**Export CSV** downloads `Branch, Franchise, PartNumber, Description, EquipMinimum, OnHand, Cost, MinimumValue, WouldOrderValue, LastRequested`.',
    ] },
    { p: 'To take a listed part off the order, fix the minimum in Equip, or set a min override of 0 on its Part page. To keep it stocked, leave it, or set the min override you want.' },

    { h2: 'Stock value with no quantity' },
    { p: 'New in 0.11.0, from Ben’s “value without quantity”. It lists parts where **Equip shows a stock value of $1 or more, either way, but nothing or less than nothing on hand**. Its conditions read “Equip stock value (either way) ≥ $1.00 and On hand ≤ 0”.' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Equip stock value** is Equip’s own figure for what the part’s stock is worth, as it shows on the part. It is **not** worked out by the tool from on hand × cost, so it can disagree with them, and it can be negative.',
      '**Either way** means a value of $1.00 or more, or −$1.00 or less. Example: a part with none on hand and $120.00 of value is listed; so is a part with −2 on hand and −$45.00.',
      '**Why it matters:** the stock value adds up into what the inventory is worth on the books. A value with no parts behind it means the books carry stock that isn’t on the shelf (or, when negative, the other way round). It usually needs a correction in Equip.',
      '**Values under $1 are left out**, either way: Equip has many penny leftovers on parts with no stock, and they would bury the ones worth fixing.',
    ] },
    { p: 'On November 2, 2026 Prod had **415** such parts. The results show **On hand**, **Cost** and **Equip stock value** (with its sign), and the total at the top is the Equip stock value of the parts listed, which can be a negative dollar figure when the negative values outweigh the positive ones.' },

    { h2: 'Open a check: filters, sort and CSV' },
    { p: 'Click a check’s name or **Open**. The page shows the check’s name (with an **Off** tag if it is switched off, and **Edit** for a saved check), its description and its conditions, and **← Cleanup checks** to go back. Every branch loads first.' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Branch** and **Franchise** at the top narrow the list (**All** = every one). Picking a branch resets the franchise.',
      'Next to them: the number of parts listed and their total value, written like “415 parts · -$1,234.56”. The value is the **Equip stock value** for Stock value with no quantity and for any check that uses Equip stock value; otherwise it is the **On-hand value (on hand × cost)**.',
      'The table: **Branch**, **Franchise**, **Part** (click it to open the Part page), **Description**, **Bin**, then a column for each field the check’s conditions use. A date that is blank shows **Never**; a part with no dealer group shows **(none)**.',
      '**Sort** by clicking a column heading; click again to reverse. While branches are still loading the list stays in branch order (“Listed by branch while the rest load; sorted once every branch has loaded.”).',
      'The first 200 parts show; **Show 200 more** at the bottom adds more.',
      '**Export CSV** downloads what the filters show, with the same columns as the table: `Branch, Franchise, PartNumber, Description, Bin`, then each field (for example `OnHand, Cost, EquipStockValue`). The file is named after the check and the data date, for example `Cleanup-Stock value with no quantity-20261102.csv`. If not every branch has loaded, the button reads **Export loaded branches (12 of 19)** and the file name ends in **-partial**.',
    ] },

    { h2: 'Build your own check' },
    { p: 'Click **New check** on the list. The editor has three cards: **Check** (name and description), **Conditions** and **Try it**. “Nothing is saved until Save check.”' },
    { steps: [
      'Type a **Name** (required) and, if you like, a **Description**: what the check is for and what to do with the parts it finds.',
      'Set **Condition 1**: pick a **field**, a **comparison** and a **value**. The value box fits the field: a number, a date, some text, or ticks. A new check starts with “On hand = 0”.',
      'Click **Add condition** for each further condition. It starts on the next field not used yet. **Remove** takes a condition out (a check always has at least one). **All conditions must match**: a part is listed only when it meets every one.',
      'Watch **Try it** (below): it shows what the check finds today, as you type.',
      'Click **Save check**. The check’s results open with “Check saved.”, and the check is on the list for everyone.',
    ], ref: 'steps11' },
    { p: '**Example** (Brian’s test of November 2): **On hand > 0** and **Sales 24 months = 0** and **Cost > 100**: parts with stock on the shelf, no sales in two years, costing over $100 each. Under the conditions the editor sums them up in one line: “On hand > 0 and Sales 24 months = 0 and Cost > $100.00”.' },
    { h3: 'The fields' },
    { table: [
      ['Field', 'What it is'],
      ['On hand', 'Equip’s quantity on hand. Can be negative.'],
      ['On order', 'Equip’s quantity on order (purchase orders and transfers in Equip).'],
      ['Pending', 'Stock committed to open tickets in Equip.'],
      ['Back order', 'Equip’s back-order quantity.'],
      ['Cost', 'The part’s cost, per unit.'],
      ['On-hand value', 'On hand (whole units, never below 0) × cost, worked out by the tool.'],
      ['Equip stock value', 'Equip’s own stock value for the part, with its sign (see Stock value with no quantity).'],
      ['Equip stock value (either way)', 'The size of Equip’s stock value whichever way it goes: −$45 counts as $45.'],
      ['Equip minimum', 'The minimum set in Equip; blank when none.'],
      ['Equip maximum', 'The maximum set in Equip; blank when none.'],
      ['Sales 12 months', 'Units sold in the last 12 months, as Equip counts them.'],
      ['Sales 24 months', 'Units sold in the last 24 months (the last 12 plus the 12 before).'],
      ['Last requested', 'The last day the part was asked for at that branch; blank = never.'],
      ['Last received', 'The last day the branch received it from a supplier; blank = never.'],
      ['Status', 'The tool’s status this morning: Out of stock, Reorder, Stocked, Not stocked, and so on (section 3).'],
      ['Franchise', 'The part’s franchise, such as BS or MM.'],
      ['Dealer group', 'The dealer group code at that branch; type **(none)** for parts with no dealer group.'],
      ['Vendor', 'The Equip vendor number, such as 1058 (leading zeros don’t matter).'],
      ['Product group', 'The product group at that branch.'],
      ['SLC', 'The part’s SLC code.'],
      ['Source', 'The part’s source code.'],
      ['Super To', 'Equip’s Super To for the part, as typed: a part number, or sometimes a note such as BOLTS. Blank when none (new in 0.15.0).'],
      ['Manual order', 'Whether the part is set to Manual order in this tool.'],
    ], widths: w(0.3, 0.7) },
    { p: 'Each branch’s own part record decides, as for rules, so a part can be on a check at one branch and not another. Only parts in the tool are checked: the non-John Deere parts with a cost above $0, at the branches the tool covers.' },
    { h3: 'The comparisons' },
    { table: [
      ['Field kind', 'Comparisons'],
      ['Numbers and dollars', '**=  ≠  <  ≤  >  ≥** and a number. Dollars compare to the cent. Equip minimum and maximum also offer **is blank** and **is not blank**.'],
      ['Dates (Last requested, Last received)', '**before**, **before, or never**, **after**, **is blank** (never), **is not blank**. The value is **months ago** (1 to 120) or **a date**.'],
      ['Text (Franchise, Dealer group, Product group, SLC, Source, Vendor)', '**is**, **is not**, **one of** (type a value and press Enter for each), and, except Vendor, **is blank** and **is not blank**. Capitals and spaces at either end don’t matter. Franchise and Dealer group suggest the values in today’s data.'],
      ['Status', '**one of**: tick the statuses.'],
      ['Manual order', '**is yes** or **is no**.'],
    ], widths: w(0.32, 0.68), keep: true },
    { p: '**“N months ago” keeps a check current.** “Last received before 24 months ago” always means 24 months before **today**, so the check needs no editing next month. Pick **a date** instead for a fixed day.' },
    { p: '**“before, or never”** counts parts that were never requested (or never received) as old. Plain **before** leaves them out. Example: “Last received before 24 months ago, or never” finds parts not received in two years, including those never received at all.' },
    { h3: 'Blanks' },
    { p: 'Some parts have a blank: no Equip minimum, never requested, no dealer group. The rules:', keepNext: true },
    { bullets: [
      '**Number comparisons skip blanks.** “Equip minimum < 5” doesn’t list parts with no minimum; use **is blank** for those.',
      '**Dates:** before and after skip “never”; **before, or never** and **is blank** include it.',
      '**“is not” on text includes blanks.** “Product group is not FILTERS” also lists parts with no product group, because a blank isn’t FILTERS. Add “Product group is not blank” to leave them out.',
      '**is** and **one of** never match a blank (except the dealer group’s **(none)**).',
    ] },
    { h3: 'Try it' },
    { p: 'While you edit, **Try it** shows “What this check finds today over every branch, from this morning’s data. Nothing is saved.”: the number of parts and the first 20, with the fields the conditions use. It updates a moment after you stop typing (“Recomputing…”). When more than 20 match: “The first 20 of 1,234 parts. Save the check to see them all.” While a condition has a problem, a red line under it says what (“Enter a number.”, “Pick at least one.”, “Enter whole months from 1 to 120.”), and Try it says “Fix the conditions’ problems to try it.”' },
    { h3: 'Saving' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Save check** is greyed out until there is a name and every condition is right. The name can’t be the same as a built-in check or another saved check that is on; Save then refuses it under the name: “A check called Old stock already exists.”',
      'Saving opens the check’s results with **Check saved.**',
      '**Cancel** goes back without saving. Leaving with unsaved changes asks first: “Leave this page? Your changes to this check aren’t saved.”',
    ] },

    { h2: 'Edit, switch off and archive' },
    { p: 'Saved checks are shared: everyone sees them and anyone can change them. Nothing is ever deleted.' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Edit** (on the list, or at the top of the check’s page) opens the editor with the check filled in. **Save check** says **Check updated.** If someone else saved the check after you opened it, it asks first: “Shannon Cole changed this check at Nov 2, 3:40 PM. Replace it with yours?” (**Replace** or **Cancel**). If it was archived meanwhile: “This check was removed or archived meanwhile, so it wasn’t saved.”',
      '**Switch off** asks “Switch off Old stock? It stays in the list, marked Off; Switch on brings it back.” An **Off** check is greyed, listed after the ones that are on, and can still be opened. Its name is free for another check. **Switch on** brings it back (unless another check that is on now has its name).',
      '**Archive** asks “Archive Old stock? It leaves the list; Show archived and Unarchive bring it back.” A line under the list says how many are hidden (“1 archived check is hidden.”). **Show archived (1)** shows them greyed with an **Archived** tag and **Unarchive**; an archived check can’t be opened or edited until it is unarchived.',
      'The built-in checks can’t be edited, switched off or archived.',
    ] },

    { h2: '“Not in today’s data yet”' },
    { p: 'Equip stock value arrived with 0.11.0’s new morning data. When the morning data doesn’t carry a field yet, a check that uses it can’t run, and the app says so rather than listing nothing:' },
    { bullets: [
      'The list shows **Not in today’s data yet** instead of a count.',
      'The check’s page says “Equip stock value isn’t in today’s data yet. It arrives with the next publish.” and “No parts are listed until then.”',
      'In the editor, the field is marked “(not in today’s data yet)” (it can still be picked and saved), and Try it says **Not in today’s data yet**.',
    ] },
    { p: 'From November 2, 2026 the morning data has Equip stock value, so this should only show if something goes wrong with a morning’s data: tell Brian.' },

    { h1: '8. Returns & scrap (0.9.0)' },
    { p: 'The **Returns & scrap** area helps with two jobs: sending slow parts **back to the vendor for credit**, and **scrapping** parts that will never sell. Both start from parts that haven’t sold in a long time, at every branch at once. Before a part goes back or into the scrap, the tool shows whether another branch has asked for it, so it can go there instead.' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Returns:** your saved vendor returns, and **New return**.',
      '**Scrap:** your saved scrap lists, and **New scrap list**.',
      '**Return & scrap rules:** each franchise’s return terms, and which parts are old enough to scrap.',
    ] },
    { p: 'The launch card says what is still open, for example “1 open return · 2 open scrap lists” (Draft or Exported; nothing when there are none).' },
    { note: 'Nothing here changes Equip. A return or scrap list is a list you export and work from: key the return or the scrap into Equip as you do today. The stock leaves the tool’s figures with the next morning’s data, once Equip shows it gone.' },

    { h2: 'Set up a return rule for a franchise' },
    { p: 'A return rule holds a franchise’s return terms, so nobody has to remember them each time. Set it up once, **with your vendor’s terms**; every new return for that franchise uses it. There is **one active return rule per franchise**.' },
    { steps: [
      'Open **Return & scrap rules**, tab **Return rules**, and click **New return rule**.',
      'Fill in the fields below. The **Try it** card under the form shows straight away what the rule would pick today.',
      'Click **Save rule**. If another rule is already active for that franchise, the app asks “Rule ‘MM annual return’ is active for MM. Make this one active instead?”: **Make this one active** switches the other one off; **Stay** keeps it.',
    ], ref: 'steps7' },
    { table: [
      ['Field', 'What it does', 'Example'],
      ['Name', 'Required. Shown in the list and on every return made with it.', 'MM annual return'],
      ['Franchise', 'The franchise the rule is for, picked from the list.', 'MM'],
      ['No sales for', 'Months, 1 to 59. A part qualifies only if it hasn’t sold at that branch in that many full months, **and** not this month either. New rules start at 24.', '24'],
      ['Not received for', 'Months, 1 to 59, or blank. Leaves out parts the branch bought from a supplier in that time: a part just bought usually can’t go back yet. Blank = no check.', '12'],
      ['Min line value', 'Dollars, or blank. A line worth less than this (qty × cost) is left out, so the return isn’t cluttered with small lines.', '$25'],
      ['Leave out Manual order', 'On = parts set to Manual order are left out (someone orders those by hand on purpose).', 'On'],
      ['Leave out minimums', 'On = parts with an Equip minimum above 0 are left out (someone wants them stocked).', 'On'],
      ['Allowance %', 'How much you may return, as a percent (0 to 100). Use your vendor’s terms.', '10'],
      ['Based on', 'What the percent is taken of: **12-month purchases**, **Stock value** or **Qualifying parts’ value** (below). Always the franchise’s, at the branches on the return.', '12-month purchases'],
      ['Credit %', 'The share of the line value (at cost) you expect back, 0 to 100. Blank = not known. Use your vendor’s terms.', '85'],
      ['Season starts, Season ends', 'MM-DD, or both blank for all year, for a vendor that takes returns only at certain times. 11-01 to 02-28 wraps the year end. Outside the season you can still make a return; the app just says so.', '03-01 and 05-31'],
      ['Active', 'Off = not used.', 'On'],
    ], widths: w(0.22, 0.58, 0.2) },
    { p: 'What the allowance is based on:', keepNext: true },
    { bullets: [
      '**12-month purchases:** what the branches on the return bought from suppliers in that franchise over the last 12 complete months, at cost. Returns to the supplier aren’t taken off. On November 2, 2026 that is November 2025 to October 2026. Example: 10% of $40,000 of purchases is a $4,000 allowance.',
      '**Stock value:** what the franchise’s stock on hand at those branches is worth at cost today.',
      '**Qualifying parts’ value:** the value of the parts the rule picks.',
    ] },
    { p: 'The rules list shows each rule’s terms in one line, for example “10% of 12-mo purchases · no sales 24 mo · not received 12 mo · credit 85% · excl. Manual order / minimums”, with its season, **Active** or **Off**, and who changed it last.' },
    { p: '**Try it.** While you edit, Try it loads every branch and shows what the draft would pick today: **Qualifying parts**, **Their value**, the **Allowance** in dollars and the **Expected credit**, then a line such as “Pre-pick ticks 12 parts, $3,950.00 of $4,000.00.”, and the 20 biggest parts with their branch, qty, value, last sold, demand elsewhere and whether the pre-pick ticks them. Nothing is saved until you click **Save rule**.' },
    { p: 'A rule that can’t be saved says why (“No sales for must be 1 to 59 months.”). **Switch off** / **Switch on** and **Archive** are on each row; rules are never deleted. A saved change applies to new returns only: saved ones keep their lines.' },

    { h2: 'Set up a scrap rule' },
    { p: 'A scrap rule says which parts are old enough to scrap. It has no allowance and no credit. Its fields are the return rule’s **Which parts** and **When** fields, with **Min unit cost** instead of Min line value: parts costing less than that are left out. New scrap rules start at **no sales for 36 months**.' },
    { bullets: [
      '**All franchises** (the first choice under Franchise) is used for every franchise that has no scrap rule of its own. One exists already: **Scrap default**, no sales for 36 months, unit cost at least $0.10.',
      '**A franchise’s own rule** replaces the All franchises rule for that franchise only. For example, a franchise whose parts go out of date faster could have its own rule with no sales for 24 months.',
      'One active rule per franchise, plus one active All franchises rule.',
    ] },

    { h2: 'Build a vendor return' },
    { steps: [
      '**Returns → New return.** The steps across the top read **Set up · Parts · Review & save**.',
      '**Set up:** pick the **Franchise** (those with a return rule come first). The rule and its terms show under it, with a link to the rules. With no rule yet it says “No return rule for MM yet · **Create one**”, which opens a new rule for that franchise. Every branch is ticked; untick any that shouldn’t be on the return. Every branch loads first; then click **Choose parts**.',
      '**Parts:** one list across the ticked branches, biggest value first. Check the ticks (below), then click **Review**.',
      '**Review & save:** the totals and any transfers, then **Save return**.',
    ], ref: 'steps8' },
    { h3: 'Which parts are listed' },
    { p: 'A part at a branch is listed when all of these are true:', keepNext: true },
    { bullets: [
      'Some of it is **free to send back**: on hand, less stock committed to open tickets, less stock on transfers saved in this app, less stock already on another open return or scrap list.',
      'It hasn’t sold at that branch for the rule’s **No sales for** months, this month included.',
      'It wasn’t received there in the rule’s **Not received for** months, if the rule has one.',
      'The line is worth at least the **Min line value**, if any, and it isn’t left out as Manual order or for a minimum, if the rule says so.',
    ] },
    { p: 'The columns are Branch, Part (with franchise and description), Bin, **Qty** (starts at all the free stock), Cost, Value, Credit, **Last sold** (the last month it sold at that branch, such as 2023-03, or “Over 5 years”), **Demand elsewhere** and **Transfer instead**.' },
    { h3: 'The pre-pick and the allowance meter' },
    { p: 'The tool ticks lines for you, up to the allowance, worst sellers first: the parts that haven’t sold for longest (never sold in 5 years first of all), and the more valuable line first among equals. A line that would go over what is left of the allowance is skipped, and a smaller one further down may still fit. So the ticks come as close to the allowance as they can without going over.' },
    { p: 'The meter above the list reads like “$3,950.00 of $4,000.00 allowance”. It turns **red** with “(over)” when your ticks go over. You can still save: the meter is a warning, because the vendor has the last word.' },
    { h3: 'Changing what goes back' },
    { bullets: [
      'Tick or untick any line. The box in the heading ticks or unticks every line shown.',
      'Lower a **Qty** to send back only some of it (a whole number, never more than is free: “At most 4”).',
      '**Search** by part or description, and pick a **Branch** to see one branch. Ticks stay when you search.',
      'The footer stays at the bottom of the window with the totals, for example “12 lines · $3,950.00 · credit $3,357.50 · 1 to transfer”, and **Back to setup** and **Review**.',
    ] },
    { h3: 'Demand elsewhere and Transfer instead' },
    { p: '**Demand elsewhere** lists the other branches that asked for the part (requests) in the last 18 complete months, busiest first: “8 (2), 13 (2), 96 (2), 94 (1)” means branch 8 asked twice, and so on. Every other branch counts, ticked or not. You can change the 18 months on Set up (**Demand window (months)**).' },
    { p: 'Where another branch wants the part, **Transfer instead** sends the line there: tick it and pick the branch. The busiest branch is chosen to start with, and the list shows only branches that asked for it and can receive it. Why: stock another branch is selling is worth more there than as a credit or as scrap. A line sent by transfer leaves the return and its totals, and becomes a **transfer order** when you save.' },
    { p: 'On a return, Transfer instead starts **off** (the demand still shows, for you to decide). “No branch can receive it” means the branches that asked for it don’t take transfers of it (their transfer rule, or they don’t carry the part).' },
    { h3: 'Saving' },
    { p: 'Review shows **Lines**, **Value at cost**, **Expected credit** and **Allowance**, and the lines to transfer instead, grouped From → To. **Save return** checks the stock again first. If someone used it meanwhile (a sale, a transfer, another list), **nothing is saved**: a banner names each part, for example “6397 at 11: 1 available now, 2 ticked”. Go back to the parts, where those lines are marked, and save again. The save makes the transfer orders first, then the return as a **Draft**, and opens it.' },

    { h2: 'Make a scrap list' },
    { p: 'The same three steps, from **Scrap → New scrap list**, using the franchise’s scrap rule (its own, or All franchises). The differences:', keepNext: true },
    { bullets: [
      'No allowance, meter or credit.',
      'Every qualifying line starts ticked.',
      '**Transfer instead starts on** for every line another branch has asked for, to the busiest branch that can receive it. Scrapping a part another branch is asking for throws away stock that could sell. Untick Transfer instead if you still want to scrap it.',
      'If every ticked line goes by transfer, Review says “Nothing left to scrap: 3 parts go to other branches.” and the button reads **Save transfers**. Only the transfer orders are saved: “Saved 1 transfer order. No scrap list was made: nothing was left to scrap.”, with a link to each.',
    ] },
    { p: '**Example** (Prod data, November 2, 2026, rule Scrap default). Franchise AM had **49** qualifying parts. Branch 11’s **6397** (BRKT): 2 at $239.62 = $479.24, last sold 2023-03, no demand elsewhere, so it stays on the scrap list. Branch 91’s **9447** (98-3/8" GANG BOLT), $490.48, was asked for at 8 (2), 13 (2), 96 (2) and 94 (1), so it starts as **Transfer instead** to branch 8 (8, 13 and 96 tie; branch order picks 8). The counts change every day.' },

    { h2: 'After saving: statuses, exports and the RA number' },
    { p: '**Returns** and **Scrap** list the saved ones, newest first: the title (franchise, RET or SCR, the date and time, such as AM-SCR-20261102-1026), franchise, branches, lines, value at cost, a return’s credit (expected / received), the status and who saved it when. **Show archived** shows the archived ones too. Click a title to open it.' },
    { table: [
      ['Status', 'What it means', 'What you can do'],
      ['Draft (amber)', 'Just saved. Its parts are held (below).', 'Lower a quantity or **Remove** a line. **Export CSV**. **Cancel**.'],
      ['Exported (blue)', 'The CSV has been downloaded. Still held.', 'Export again. **Mark sent to vendor** (return) or **Mark scrapped** (scrap list). **Cancel**.'],
      ['Sent to vendor (green)', 'Return only: the parts have gone back.', '**Credit received**: enter the amount (it starts at the expected credit) and **Save credit**.'],
      ['Credit received (dark green)', 'Return only: done. The page shows, for example, “Expected $4,250.00 · Received $3,980.00 (94%)”.', 'Archive.'],
      ['Scrapped (green)', 'Scrap list only: done.', 'Archive.'],
      ['Cancelled (grey)', 'Not going ahead. Its parts are free for other lists.', 'Archive.'],
    ], widths: w(0.22, 0.4, 0.38), keep: true },
    { bullets: [
      '**RA number** (returns): type the vendor’s return authorization number and press Enter: “RA number saved.” It can be changed at any time.',
      '**Export CSV** downloads one file: `Branch, PartNumber, Description, Bin, Qty, Cost, LineValue, CreditPct, CreditValue, LastSold` and a Total row (a scrap list has no credit columns). **Export by branch** (when there is more than one branch) downloads one file per branch, to use as pick lists. Either marks it Exported.',
      '**Mark sent to vendor** and **Mark scrapped** need an export first, because the CSV is the record of what went.',
      '**Cancel** (Draft or Exported) asks first: “Cancel this return? Its parts are no longer held and can go on another return.”',
      '**Archive** hides a finished one (sent, credited, scrapped or cancelled). Nothing is deleted; **Unarchive** brings it back.',
      '**Transfer orders made with it** lists the transfer orders saved for its Transfer instead lines, with their status. They are ordinary transfer orders: export them and mark them entered in Equip on the **Transfers** page as usual. Cancelling the return or scrap list doesn’t cancel them.',
      'If someone else changed it while you had it open, your change is refused: “This return was changed by someone else; reload it.”',
    ] },

    { h2: '“On return” and “On scrap” tags' },
    { p: 'While a return or scrap list is **Draft** or **Exported**, its parts are **held in the app**: they aren’t offered on another return or scrap list, and a grey tag shows wherever the part appears, for example **On scrap AM-SCR-20261102-1026 (2)**: the list and the quantity it holds. Click the tag to open the list. The tags show on:', keepNext: true },
    { bullets: [
      'Recommended Reorder lines;',
      'transfer suggestions, by the sending branch: Reorder step 1, the Transfers page and One-Time Order’s Transfers step;',
      'the Part page’s **Other branches** table.',
    ] },
    { p: 'The hold is in the app only. **Equip isn’t changed**, and orders and transfer suggestions are worked out as before, so a tagged part can still be suggested as a transfer: check the tag before saving one. The hold ends when the list is sent to the vendor, scrapped or cancelled. Key the return or scrap into Equip promptly: until the next morning’s data shows the stock gone, the tool still counts it.' },

    { h1: '9. One-Time templates (0.10.0)' },
    { p: 'A **template** saves a One-Time Order’s Set up under a name, so an order you make every season starts with one click. It holds:' },
    { bullets: [
      'the **branches**, **franchises** and **dealer groups** (none ticked = every group);',
      'the **months**, kept as a period (see “Periods stay current” below);',
      'the **Calculation**: Total or Avg / mo, the loading factor (or months of supply), min demands and min sales;',
      'the **Min line value** and **Max line value**;',
      'a **name** and an optional **description**.',
    ] },
    { p: 'It doesn’t hold the order name (that belongs to each order) or any transfers: the Transfers step works those out fresh every time, from that day’s stock.' },
    { p: 'Templates are **shared**: everyone sees the same ones, and anyone can save, change or archive one. Every change is recorded with who made it and when, and nothing is ever deleted. You work with them in two places: the **Template** card at the top of One-Time Order’s Set up (“Start from a saved set-up, or save this one for next time.”) and the **Templates** page in the menu, right under One-Time Order.' },

    { h2: 'Save a Set up as a template' },
    { steps: [
      'Set up a One-Time Order as usual: branches, franchises, any dealer groups, the months, the Calculation and any line value limits.',
      'On the **Template** card, click **Save as template**. (It stays greyed out until the branches have loaded, a month is picked and the Calculation has no problems.)',
      'Type a **Template name** (it starts as the order name) and, if you like, a **Description**, such as who it is for or when it is used. Click **Save**.',
    ], ref: 'steps9' },
    { p: 'The name must be different from every other template that isn’t archived (capitals and spaces at either end don’t count), and at most 80 characters. The form says what’s wrong, for example “Give the template a name.” or “A template called "Cotton pre-season" already exists.” The template’s history starts with **Created**.' },

    { h2: 'Start an order from a template' },
    { steps: [
      'Open **One-Time Order**. On the **Template** card, pick the template under **Start from a template** and click **Use**. (Or click **Start order** on the Templates page, which opens One-Time Order and does the same.)',
      'The app ticks the template’s branches first and says “Starting from Cotton pre-season. Waiting for the branches to load.”, because franchises and dealer groups can only be ticked once their branches have loaded. **Cancel** stops it; so does changing the branch ticks yourself.',
      'Then it fills in the rest: franchises, dealer groups, the months, the Calculation and the line value limits. If the **Order name** was blank, it gets the template’s name; a name you already typed is kept.',
      'The card now says **From template Cotton pre-season**, with its description. Check the Set up and carry on as usual: **Continue to transfers**, then Review and **Create orders**.',
    ], ref: 'steps10' },
    { p: '**When something isn’t in today’s data.** The app never changes a template by itself, and never refuses to start from one. It fills in what today’s data has and lists what it left out in a blue box (**Dismiss** closes it), for example:', keepNext: true },
    { bullets: [
      '“Branch 97 isn’t in today’s data, so it was left out.” (the same for a franchise or a dealer group);',
      '“Nov 2026 hasn’t finished yet, so it was left out.”, or a month older than the history (about 5 years) left out with a note saying so.',
    ] },
    { p: '**Dealer groups that are all gone.** If none of the template’s dealer groups is in today’s data, the order does **not** quietly widen to every group. The note says “Dealer group COTTON isn’t in today’s data. Pick the dealer groups for this order.”, and Set up waits until you tick the groups you want.' },

    { h2: 'Change a template' },
    { p: 'Templates are changed from One-Time Order’s Set up: start from the template, change the Set up, then save it back.' },
    { bullets: [
      'As soon as the Set up differs from the template, a line under the buttons says how, for example “Not saved to the template yet: loading factor 1.5 → 2”, and **Update template** lights up. Changing it back makes the line go away.',
      '**Update template** saves your Set up into the same template, keeping its name and description. It says “Template updated.”, and the history gets a line such as “loading factor 1.5 → 2”.',
      '**Save as new template** leaves the template as it was and saves your Set up as another one. The name starts as “Cotton pre-season (copy)”: change it.',
      'If some of the template’s branches, franchises, dealer groups or months were left out today (above), **Update template** saves without them, and the card says so: “Updating also removes what today’s data left out (see the notes above).” Use **Save as new template** to keep the original as it was.',
      '**Archive** (on the card) archives the template you are using, after asking. **Stop using** unhooks the Set up from the template without changing anything: the Set up stays as it is, and orders made from it no longer say which template they came from.',
    ] },
    { p: '**Someone else changed it meanwhile.** **Update template** reads the template again before saving. If someone saved it after you started from it, it asks first, for example “Shannon Cole changed this template on Nov 2, 10:14 AM (loading factor 1.5 → 2). Replace it with yours?”. **Replace** saves your Set up over theirs (their change stays in the history); **Cancel** saves nothing, so you can look first, or use Save as new template. If someone archived it meanwhile, it isn’t updated: “Someone archived this template, so it can’t be updated. Save it as a new template instead.”' },

    { h2: 'History and “Changed by …”' },
    { p: '**History** (on the card, and on each row of the Templates page) lists every change, newest first, one per line: when, who and what, for example “Nov 2, 10:14 AM · Shannon Cole · loading factor 1.5 → 2”. The first line is **Created**; **Archived** and **Unarchived** are recorded too. Several changes saved at once are joined with “;”, for example “added franchise KU; period Rolling 12 → Rolling 24”.' },
    { p: 'When you start from a template that someone else has changed since you last started from it **on this computer**, the card says so: “Changed by Shannon Cole on Nov 2, 10:14 AM”, with a **History** link to see what changed. Your own changes don’t show it, and neither does the first time you use a template on a computer.' },

    { h2: 'Periods stay current' },
    { p: 'A template keeps the months as a **period**, not as fixed dates, so a template saved today still means the same thing next season. When you save, the app works out the period from the months ticked:', keepNext: true },
    { table: [
      ['You ticked', 'The template keeps', 'When you start from it later'],
      ['The latest N complete months (Rolling 12, 24, 36 or any number)', '**Rolling N**', 'The N latest complete months on that day. Rolling 12 stays rolling: used in March 2027 it means March 2026 to February 2027.'],
      ['Every month of one calendar year', '**Full year**, for example Full year 2025', 'Always January to December of that year.'],
      ['Any other months', '**Those months**, for example May 2026, Mar 2026', 'Always those months. Once one is older than the history (about 5 years), it is left out with a note.'],
    ], widths: w(0.3, 0.25, 0.45), keep: true },
    { p: 'One catch: in January, the latest 12 months are exactly last year, so a **new** template saved then from last year’s months is kept as **Rolling 12**. A template that is already Full year 2025 stays Full year 2025 when you update it in January.' },

    { h2: 'The Templates page' },
    { p: '**Templates** in the Non-JD ordering menu (right under One-Time Order) lists every template: the **Name** with its description, the **Set up** in one line (for example “Branches 1, 92 · BS · Rolling 12 · total units sold × 1.5”), and **Changed** (who saved it last, and when). Each row has:', keepNext: true },
    { bullets: [
      '**Start order:** opens One-Time Order with the template filled in, as **Use** does.',
      '**History:** opens the change list under the row; click it again to close it.',
      '**Archive:** asks “Archive Cotton pre-season? It leaves One-Time Order’s template list; Unarchive brings it back.” An archived template isn’t offered on One-Time Order and has no Start order, but nothing is deleted and its history stays.',
      '**Show archived (N)** at the top (only when some are archived) shows them greyed, with an **Archived** tag and an **Unarchive** button. While it is off, a line under the list says how many are hidden.',
    ] },
    { p: 'The page doesn’t edit templates: start from one, change the Set up and use **Update template**. An archived template keeps its name, so a new template can reuse it; unarchiving the old one then says “Rename the other Cotton pre-season first.” There is no rename button yet: archive the other one first if you want the old one back.' },

    { h2: 'Orders say which template they came from' },
    { p: 'An order made from a template records it. Review’s line about the calculation, and the same line on the saved order’s page (**Orders** → open it), end with “· From template Cotton pre-season”. Orders made after **Stop using** don’t.' },

    { h1: '10. Inventory reports (0.12.0)' },
    { p: 'The **Inventory management** area brings the **Corp Parts** Excel workbooks into the app as **reports**. A report lists the JD parts, at every branch at once, that meet its conditions. The first one is **MLPF Gaps**. It uses the same rules and columns as MLPF Gaps.xlsx, so it lists the same parts.' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Which parts:** every **active** JD (franchise D) part at every branch: something on hand, something on order, a sale in the last 24 months, or a Corp demand. On November 3, 2026 that was **250,867** part records at **27** branches. Unlike the rest of the tool, this includes the branches it leaves out (such as 12); each report leaves out what its workbook leaves out.',
      '**Which day:** the morning data, as the rest of the tool. Each report says **Data as of …**. The workbook reads Equip live, so a sale or receipt made today shows in the workbook but not in the app until tomorrow.',
    ] },
    { note: 'Nothing here changes Equip, and the workbooks keep working. A report is a list you filter, export and work through.' },

    { h2: 'The list of reports' },
    { p: 'Open **Inventory management** from the launch page (or the Area select); **Reports** in the menu. The page is **Inventory reports**: “The Corp Parts reports over the JD parts, every branch. Open one to filter it and export a CSV.” The first visit loads every JD branch (“Loading JD parts: 12 of 27 branches…”); this takes a few seconds. Each row shows:' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Report:** the name (click it to open the report), the description, and its conditions in one line.',
      '**By:** **—** for the built-in; for a copy, who made it and who changed it last.',
      '**Parts:** how many parts it finds over every branch. The counts change every morning.',
      'The buttons: **Open** and **Copy as my own** on every report; **Edit**, **Switch off** / **Switch on** and **Archive** on copies.',
    ] },
    { p: '**MLPF Gaps** comes first, marked **🔒 Built in**, then the copies that are on (A to Z), then any that are switched off.' },

    { h2: 'MLPF Gaps' },
    { p: 'The built-in report, from the MLPF Gaps workbook: “JD parts with exactly 2 Corp demands and nothing on hand or on order, returnable, package qty 1, not superseded (the MLPF Gaps workbook).” A part at a branch is listed when **all** of these are true:' },
    { bullets: [
      'It has **exactly 2 Corp demands** at that branch (see below).',
      '**Nothing on hand** (less than 1) and **nothing on order**.',
      'It is **returnable** (return indicator R).',
      'Its **package qty is 1** (sold one at a time, not in a pack).',
      'It is **not superseded** (Super To is blank).',
      'The branch is **not 12** (the Combine Vault).',
      'Its source is **not AN**.',
      'Its SLC **doesn’t start with 21, 90, 91 or 99**.',
    ] },
    { p: 'The page shows them in one line: “Branch is not 12 and Corp demands = 2 and On hand < 1 and On order = 0 and Package qty = 1 and Return indicator is R and Source is not AN and SLC doesn’t start with 21, 90, 91, 99 and Super To is blank”. On November 3, 2026 (Prod) it found about **10,090** parts.' },
    { p: 'The built-in never changes. It can’t be edited, switched off or archived, only copied.' },

    { h3: 'The Corp demand rule' },
    { p: 'All the Corp Parts workbooks count demand the same way, and so does the app:', keepNext: true },
    { bullets: [
      'One demand = **one customer invoice** for the part at that branch. An invoice with several lines for the part, or a quantity of 5, is still one demand.',
      'The invoice is dated between **7 days and 18 months** ago. The last week is left out.',
      '**Credits are netted** against the invoice they return. An invoice that is **fully credited** back doesn’t count; a partial return still counts.',
      '**Transfers never count**, nor purchases, receipts or adjustments.',
    ] },
    { p: 'Example: a part sold on two invoices in the last year, one of them later credited in full, has 1 Corp demand, so it is not on MLPF Gaps.' },

    { h2: 'Use the table' },
    { p: 'Click a report’s name or **Open**. The page shows its name, description, conditions and **Data as of …**, with **← Inventory reports** at the top to go back.' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Branch** at the top narrows the list to one branch (**All branches** to start with).',
      '**Search** finds a part number or description containing what you type (capitals don’t matter).',
      'The **columns** are the workbook’s: Branch, Part number, Description, Cost, Source, SLC, Commodity code, Commodity group, Dealer group, On order, On hand, Corp demands. A blank shows **—**; a part with no dealer group shows **(none)**.',
      '**Sort** by clicking a column heading: the first click puts the largest (or Z) first, the second reverses it. To start with, the list is sorted by part number, then branch, as the workbook is.',
      'Next to the filters: the number of parts shown and **Total cost (1 each)**: the cost of one of each part listed. MLPF parts have nothing on hand, so this is what stocking one of each would cost.',
      'The first 200 parts show; **Show 200 more** at the bottom adds more.',
      '**Click a part** (or anywhere on its row) to open its details on the right: every field the app has for it, such as Package qty, Return indicator, Equip minimum and maximum, and sales. **Close** or Escape closes it.',
      '**Export CSV** downloads what the filters show, in the table’s order. The headings are the workbook’s (Branch, PartNumber, Description, Cost, Source, SLC, CommodityCode, CommodityGroup, DealerGroupCode, OnOrder, OnHandQty, Demands), so the file lines up beside MLPF Gaps.xlsx. The file is named like `Inventory-MLPF Gaps-20261103.csv`.',
    ] },

    { h2: 'Copy as my own' },
    { p: 'To change a report, make your own copy. The built-in stays as it is.' },
    { bullets: [
      'Click **Copy as my own** on the list or on the report’s page. The editor (**Copy report**) opens with the report’s conditions, the name “Copy of MLPF Gaps” and the description “Based on MLPF Gaps.”. Change the name to say what your version is for.',
      'Change the conditions as in a Cleanup check (section 7): pick a field, a comparison and a value; **Add condition** or **Remove**. All conditions must match. For example, change **Corp demands = 2** to **Corp demands ≥ 2** to see parts with 2 or more demands.',
      '**Try it** shows what your version finds as you type: the number of parts and the first 20.',
      'Click **Save report**. Your copy opens with “Report saved.” and is on the list for everyone. Its table and CSV have the workbook’s columns, then any other field your conditions use (for example Package qty, Return indicator, Super To).',
      '**Edit** changes a copy later (“Report updated.”). A name can’t repeat MLPF Gaps or another copy that is on: “A report called … already exists.”',
      '**Switch off** keeps a copy on the list, greyed and marked **Off**. **Archive** takes it off the list; **Show archived** and **Unarchive** bring it back. Nothing is deleted.',
    ] },
    { p: 'The fields a copy can use: Branch, Part number, Description, Corp demands, On hand, On order, Cost, On-hand value (0.15.0), Package qty, Return indicator, Source, SLC, Super To, Commodity code, Commodity group, Dealer group, Equip minimum, Equip maximum, Sales 12 months and Sales 24 months. Text fields also offer **starts with** and **doesn’t start with**, with a list of starts: “SLC doesn’t start with 21, 90, 91, 99”. A blank never starts with anything, so “doesn’t start with” keeps blanks.' },

    { h2: '“JD parts aren’t in today’s data yet”' },
    { p: 'The JD parts came with the morning data of November 3, 2026. If a morning’s data doesn’t carry them, the Inventory pages say “JD parts aren’t in today’s data yet. They arrive with the next publish.” and show nothing else. The rest of the app works as normal. If you see it after November 3, tell Brian.' },
    { p: 'If one branch’s parts don’t load, the page names it, for example “Branch 4 didn’t load; the count covers the rest.” (with the branch’s name), with **Try again**. The CSV is then named **-partial**.' },

    { h1: '11. Border Stores and 2 & 4 Cotton (0.13.0)' },
    { p: 'Two more Corp Parts workbooks are now built-in reports: **Border Stores** and **2 & 4 Cotton** (the 2 & 4 Cotton & Stripper Parts workbook). Both compare a part at one branch with the **same part at the other branches**: a part is listed when other branches are selling it and this branch has none. Like MLPF Gaps, each uses its workbook’s rules and columns, so it lists the same parts on the same morning.' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Other-branch demands:** the Corp demands of the same part at the other branches that count, added up. The row’s own branch is never one of them.',
      '**Other branches with demand:** how many of those branches have at least 1 Corp demand for it.',
      'Each branch counts once. Which branches count is the report’s **Count other branches where** list (below).',
      'Both are columns after Corp demands, and in the part’s details panel. The CSV headings are the workbooks’: … Demands, **OtherBranchDemands**, **OtherLocationCount**.',
    ] },

    { h2: 'Border Stores' },
    { p: 'The built-in: “Parts with at least 1 Corp demand at 95, 91 or 96 but nothing on hand there, that 5 or more other branches (not 2 or 4) are selling, 10 or more Corp demands between them; returnable, package qty 1, nothing on order, not superseded (the Border Stores workbook).” A part at a branch is listed when **all** of these are true:' },
    { bullets: [
      'The branch is **95, 91 or 96**, the part has **at least 1 Corp demand** there and **nothing on hand** (less than 1).',
      '**Other-branch demands is 10 or more** and **Other branches with demand is 5 or more**.',
      'The six part rules of MLPF Gaps: nothing on order, package qty 1, returnable (R), source not AN, SLC doesn’t start with 21, 90, 91 or 99, not superseded.',
    ] },
    { p: 'The other branches that count are every branch **except 2 and 4** (and the row’s own) where the part passes the same six part rules. On November 4, 2026 (Prod) it found **1,576** parts.' },

    { h2: '2 & 4 Cotton' },
    { p: 'The built-in: “Cotton, stripper and picker parts with no Corp demands and nothing on hand at 2 or 4 (parts with no activity too), that 5 or more other branches (not 2 or 4) are selling, 10 or more Corp demands between them; …”. It is Border Stores with three changes:' },
    { bullets: [
      'The branch is **2 or 4** and the part has **no Corp demands** there.',
      'It must be a **cotton part**, both at the row’s branch and at the other branches that count: its dealer group is **COTTON**, **STRIPPER** or **PICKER**, **or** the part is coded **690**, **770**, **CS690** or **JDCS690** at any branch. So a part coded COMBINE at 2 but 770 at another branch counts.',
      'It includes **parts with no activity** (below): most of its parts have never been stocked or sold at 2 or 4.',
    ] },
    { p: 'On November 4, 2026 (Prod) it found **251** parts. On the list it shows **Count (loads more data)** instead of a number; press it for the count. It loads the parts with no activity, about 11 MB the first time in a session.' },

    { h2: 'Parts with no activity' },
    { p: 'The reports normally run over the **active** JD parts (section 10). A JD part record with nothing on hand, nothing on order, no sales in 24 months and no Corp demand is a part **with no activity**: on November 4 there were about **643,000** of them. A report that needs them (2 & 4 Cotton, or a copy with **Include parts with no activity** on) loads them when you open it: “Loading the parts with no activity this report needs…”. MLPF Gaps and Border Stores never load them.' },

    { h2: 'Changing the other branches in a copy' },
    { p: '**Copy as my own** on Border Stores or 2 & 4 Cotton opens the editor with **two** lists of conditions:' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Rows to show** (“All conditions must match.”): which branch rows the report lists, as in section 10. To look at different branches, change the **Branch** condition here, for example to **one of 7, 14**. The two thresholds, **Other-branch demands ≥ 10** and **Other branches with demand ≥ 5**, are here too, right after Corp demands.',
      '**Count other branches where:** “Optional. Which of the part’s rows at other branches count towards Other-branch demands and Other branches with demand. All conditions must match.” In the built-ins it starts **Branch not one of 2, 4**. Change it to leave more branches out, or to count only some.',
      '**Include parts with no activity:** “Adds JD parts with no stock, orders, sales or demand (loads more data).” On in 2 & 4 Cotton.',
    ] },
    { p: 'Example: Brian’s test copy of Border Stores with **Branch one of 7, 14** and parts with no activity on found **1,027** parts on November 4.' },
    { p: 'A report without **Count other branches where** can’t use the two other-branch fields; **Add ‘Count other branches where’** adds the list. Copies have three more fields: **Other-branch demands**, **Other branches with demand** and **Dealer group at any branch** (true when the part has one of the codes at any branch, active or not). Text fields also offer **not one of**: “Branch not one of 2, 4”.' },

    { h3: '“Any of” groups' },
    { p: '**Add ‘any of’ group** puts a box **Any of these** (“At least one line must match.”) in either list: the group passes when **one** of its lines does, where the rest of the list needs **all**. The cotton rule is one: any of (Dealer group one of COTTON, STRIPPER, PICKER; Dealer group at any branch one of 690, 770, CS690, JDCS690). A group has 2 to 10 lines; with one line left, **Make it a plain condition** turns it back into an ordinary condition. Cleanup checks (section 7) have the same button.' },

    { h2: 'Compared with the workbooks (November 3 and 4, 2026)' },
    { p: 'Brian refreshed both workbooks at about 5 pm on November 3, and they were compared with the app’s rules over the next morning’s data. **Border Stores:** 1,573 of 1,575 rows matched. **2 & 4 Cotton:** all 248 rows matched, plus 1 extra in the app. Every difference was an order placed or a transfer received at another branch after 5 pm, except one: a few part records are stored in Equip in lower case (for example tca24616 at branch 8), and the app missed their Corp demands. That was **fixed** before release.' },

    { h1: '12. Corporate Stocking: one row per part (0.14.0)' },
    { p: 'The SPI Corparate Stocking workbook is now a built-in report, **Corporate Stocking**: “Parts with nothing on hand or on order at any branch (except 2 and 4) that had a Corp demand at 3 or more branches.” Unlike the other reports it lists **one row per part**, not one per branch: a part is looked at over every branch at once. It uses the workbook’s rules and columns, so it lists the same parts on the same morning.' },
    { p: 'A part is listed when **all** of these are true:' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Records that count:** its JD records at branches other than 2 and 4 with package qty 1, return indicator R, source not AN and an SLC that doesn’t start with 21, 90, 91 or 99. A record that fails these doesn’t count either way. The part needs at least one counted record.',
      '**Every counted record** has **0 on hand**, **0 on order** (a negative fails too) and **no Super To**. Parts with no activity (section 11) count here: a Super To at a branch that has never stocked the part still keeps it off the list.',
      '**Demand:** it had a Corp demand at **3 or more branches** other than 2 and 4, whatever those branches’ records look like. Each branch counts once.',
    ] },
    { p: 'Columns, as in the workbook: Part number, Description, Cost, Source, SLC, Commodity code, Dealer group, On order, On hand, **R18 demands** (the Corp demands added up), **Branches with demand** and **Locations with demand** (the branch codes, for example “1, 7, 14, 93”). The text columns take the largest value over the counted records. The list is sorted by Branches with demand (most first), then R18 demands, then part number. On November 5, 2026 (Prod) it found **1,815** parts.' },
    { p: 'On the list it shows a **Count (loads more data)** button, as 2 & 4 Cotton does, because it always loads the parts with no activity.' },

    { h2: 'Using the report' },
    { bullets: [
      'There is no Branch column, so the branch filter is **Demand at branch** instead: pick a branch to see only the parts with a demand there.',
      '**Search** (part number or description), sorting, the count and **Total cost (1 each)** work as in the other reports.',
      '**Click a part number:** a panel lists **every branch’s record** of the part: On hand, On order, Super To, Corp demands, **Counted** and **Demand counted**. Counted says **Yes**, or why not: “No: not in Records that count” or “No: fails Every counted record must”. A ✓ under Demand counted means that branch’s demands are in R18 demands, even when its record didn’t count. For example, a source AN record at 93 doesn’t count, but its demands do.',
      '**Export CSV** uses the workbook’s headings: PartNumber, Description, Cost, Source, SLC, CommodityCode, DealerGroupCode, OnOrder, OnHandQty, R18 Demands, Count of Locations with Demands, Locations with Demands. Copies keep these headings.',
    ] },

    { h2: 'Changing it in a copy' },
    { p: '**Copy as my own** on Corporate Stocking opens the editor with **four** cards, in this order:' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Records that count:** “Which of the part’s records, at any branch, make up the part.” Built in: Branch not one of 2, 4 and the four part rules. To stop counting branch 12, add it to the Branch condition here.',
      '**Every counted record must:** “The part is shown only when each counted record matches.” Built in: On hand = 0, On order = 0, Super To is blank.',
      '**Demand counts where:** “Which records’ Corp demands count; each branch counts once.” Built in: Branch not one of 2, 4.',
      '**Parts to show:** “Conditions on the part as a whole.” Built in: Branches with demand ≥ 3. Its fields are the part’s: R18 demands, Branches with demand, Locations with demand (for example contains 93), and the part’s Description, Cost, Source, SLC, Commodity code and group, and Dealer group.',
    ] },
    { p: 'The first three may be empty (“Every record counts.” and so on); Parts to show needs at least one condition. ‘Any of’ groups work in every card. Example: Brian’s test copy with **Branches with demand ≥ 4** found **618** parts on November 5.' },

    { h2: 'A new report' },
    { p: '**New report** (top right of Inventory reports, above “Data as of”) opens a blank editor. It starts with **Report type**: **One row per branch** (“A part at a branch: each branch’s record is a row.”, like MLPF Gaps) or **One row per part** (“A part over every branch, parts with no activity too: each part is a row.”, like Corporate Stocking). A copy keeps its report’s type.' },

    { h2: 'Compared with the workbook (November 5, 2026)' },
    { p: 'Brian refreshed the workbook at 8:01 am on November 5, and it was compared with the app’s rules over that morning’s data. **1,817** parts matched on every column. Five more parts in Excel had one more demand at branch 94, from an invoice posted between the app’s data time (6:42 am) and the refresh; one part had just left Excel’s 18-month window; two had changed at branch 11 in Equip after JD’s overnight copy (checked by Brian). For 4 parts the description differs: where branches spell a description differently, Equip’s SQL picks one of them in a way the app can’t copy exactly. Brian accepted that. The app’s CSV then matched the reference exactly: **1,815** parts.' },

    { h1: '13. Stock on superseded parts (0.15.0)' },
    { p: 'Ben’s request of November 5: stock still sitting under an old part number. It is a built-in in **two places**, with the same rule: **on hand more than 0 and Super To not blank**, one row per branch record. JD parts are an Inventory report; non-JD parts are a Cleanup check. “On hand” is Equip’s quantity on hand, so stock anywhere at the branch counts, not only the main bin.' },
    { bullets: [
      '**Any Super To counts**, notes such as **BOLTS** too, so you can see them and decide. A Super To of only spaces counts as blank.',
      '**Inventory:** the fifth built-in, under Corporate Stocking, a separate report (Corporate Stocking’s rules are unchanged). It counts at once, with **no Count button**: parts with no activity have no stock, so it never needs them. Columns: Branch, Part number, Description, On hand, Cost, **On-hand value**, Super To, Source, SLC. It starts in part number order; click **On-hand value** to sort by it. **Copy as my own** works as on the other reports.',
      '**On-hand value** is new for Inventory reports: on hand (whole units, never below 0) × cost, the same as Cleanup’s. Copies can use it, and the panel you get by clicking a part now shows it too.',
      '**Cleanup:** the third built-in check. Columns after the part: On hand, Cost, On-hand value, Super To; the total is the on-hand value. **Super To** is also a new field for your own checks (section 7).',
    ] },
    { p: 'On November 5, 2026 (Prod): JD **2,477** records at 20 branches, **$571,028.49** on hand; non-JD **698** records at 19 branches, **$497,322.78**, 22 of them with a word Super To. Both CSVs matched an independent list from the same data record for record.' },

    { h1: '14. During the test' },
    { bullets: [
      'This is a test copy on a test server; the address and the certificate warning will change for the real version.',
      'Orders and settings you save are real (they go into the SharePoint lists), but nothing is sent to a vendor; you download the CSV and use it yourself.',
      'Please note anything confusing, missing or wrong, even small wording, in the **Anything else** box of the questions document, or send me an email.',
    ] },
  ],
});
